T = {
# ---------- Mate in 1 ----------
'hZK5p': ("Look at Black's back rank.",
  "<b>Rxe8#</b>. The bishop on e8 is the only piece guarding Black's back rank. The rook takes it with check, and the king is boxed in by its own pawns on f7, g7 and h7. This is the classic <i>back-rank mate</i>."),
'dFeRT': ("Black has just taken your queen. Look at f7.",
  "<b>Bxf7#</b>. Black has just won the queen on d1, but it is too late. The bishop takes on f7 with check, protected by the knight on e5. The king cannot go to d7 because the knight covers it, and Black's own pieces block d8, e7 and f8."),
'I7RL3': ("Even a pawn can give checkmate.",
  "<b>exf7#</b>. The pawn captures the knight and checks the king on g8. The knight on g5 protects the pawn, so the king cannot take it, and Black's own bishop, rook and pawns fill every escape square."),
'RAlIG': ("Which piece protects h7?",
  "<b>Qxh7#</b>. The queen captures Black's queen on h7 with check. The knight on g5 protects it, so the king cannot take; the queen also covers g7, and Black's own rook blocks g8."),
'wEF2Y': ("Find a protected square next to White's king.",
  "<b>...Qxb2#</b>. The queen captures on b2 with check. The knight on c4 protects the queen, so the king cannot take it. The queen and knight cover b1, c2 and d2, and White's own queen blocks d1."),
'TJZuN': ("Your bishop on h5 is already helping.",
  "<b>...Rd1#</b>. The rook checks along the d-file, and the bishop on h5 protects it, so the king cannot capture. The rook also controls c1 and e1, the bishop covers e2, and White's own bishop and rook block c3 and e3."),
# ---------- Mate in 2 ----------
'3uVpS': ("Which black piece is the only defender of the back rank?",
  "<b>1.Qxc8+! Bxc8 2.Re8#</b>. The queen sacrifice forces the bishop on e6 to capture, and that opens the e-file. The rook reaches e8, and the king is trapped by its own pawns and the rook on h8. (If 1...Qd8, then 2.Qxd8#.)"),
'lq5yC': ("Remove the defender of h7.",
  "<b>1.Bh7+! Nxh7 2.Qxh7#</b>. The bishop check pulls the knight away from f6, where it was guarding h7. Then the queen takes on h7, protected by the knight on g5, while the bishop on a3 covers f8. (If 1...Kh8, then 2.Nxf7# is mate.)"),
'A3TsZ': ("Capture first, then use a double check.",
  "<b>1.Rxf8+ Kh7 2.Ng5#</b>. After the rook takes on f8, the knight jumps to g5 and gives <i>double check</i>: from the knight and from the bishop on d3. In a double check, only the king can move, and every square is covered. (2.Nf6# also mates.)"),
'49aOn': ("Check with the bishop first.",
  "<b>1.Bg6+ Kf8 2.Qf7#</b>. The bishop check forces the king to f8, and then the queen lands on f7, protected by the bishop. The king has no square left."),
'ROobg': ("Look for a smothered mate.",
  "<b>1...Qa1+ 2.Nb1 Na2#</b>. The queen check forces White to block with the knight. Then ...Na2 is mate: White's king on c1 is <i>smothered</i>, completely surrounded by its own pieces."),
'nC36n': ("Your knight on g4 controls f2 and h2.",
  "<b>1...Qf2+ 2.Kh3 Qxh2#</b>. The queen checks from f2, protected by the knight on g4. Wherever the king goes, ...Qxh2 is mate, again protected by the knight. (2.Kh1 Qxh2# is the same.)"),
# ---------- Forks ----------
'defui': ("A knight check can hit two pieces at once.",
  "<b>1.Nxa7+ Kb8 2.Nxc6+</b>. The knight captures on a7 with check and at the same time attacks the queen on c6. After the king moves, White wins the queen. This double attack is a <i>fork</i>."),
'oorYJ': ("Which square attacks the king and the rook together?",
  "<b>1.Qe6+ Kf8 2.Qxf5</b>. The queen checks the king and attacks the loose rook on f5. Black must get out of check, and then White wins the rook."),
'EoeiW': ("The knight on d5 is undefended.",
  "<b>1.Qf5+ Kg8 2.Qxd5</b>. From f5 the queen checks the king on h7 and attacks the undefended knight on d5. Whatever Black does about the check, the knight falls."),
'xX7Uc': ("The famous knight check on c2.",
  "<b>1...Nc2+ 2.Kd1 Nxa1</b>. The knight checks the king on e1 and attacks the rook on a1. Wherever the king goes, the rook falls. This is one of the most common forks in chess."),
'u8B5b': ("A rook can fork too.",
  "<b>1...Rb1+ 2.Kh2 Rxa1</b>. The rook checks along the first rank and attacks the queen on a1. It is protected by the other rook on b8, so 2.Qxb1 Rxb1+ doesn't help White. Black wins the queen."),
'79aPu': ("Check the king and hit the bishop on b5.",
  "<b>1...Qa5+ 2.c3 Qxb5</b>. The queen checks the king on e1 along the diagonal and attacks the bishop on b5 along the rank. Whether White blocks or moves the king, Black wins the bishop."),
# ---------- Pins and skewers ----------
'V1uLq': ("Check the king and look behind it.",
  "<b>1.Re2+ Kxf5 2.Rxe7</b>. The rook checks the king on e4. The king has to move off the e-file, and then the rook behind it on e7 is lost. Attacking a valuable piece with a less valuable one behind it is a <i>skewer</i>."),
'AQfUV': ("The king and the knight stand on the same diagonal.",
  "<b>1.Bf5+ Kf4 2.Bxc2</b>. The bishop checks the king on e4. When the king steps aside, the bishop captures the knight on c2, which was standing behind it on the same diagonal."),
'GOvJ9': ("The queen and the king are on the same diagonal.",
  "<b>1.Bg5!</b> The bishop <i>pins</i> the queen on e7 to the king on d8, so the queen is not allowed to move away. Taking the bishop with 1...Qxg5 loses the queen to 2.Qxg5+. White wins the queen for a bishop, e.g. <b>1...Kc7 2.Bxe7</b>."),
'5b82J': ("Check from behind on the a-file.",
  "<b>1...Ra8+ 2.Kb5 Rxa2</b>. The rook checks the king on a5. The king must leave the a-file, and the rook captures White's rook on a2. A rook-endgame skewer worth remembering."),
'Kxbcj': ("White's queen and king share a diagonal.",
  "<b>1...Bc5!</b> The bishop pins White's queen on d4 to the king on g1. Defending the queen with <b>2.Be3</b> does not help: after <b>2...Bxd4 3.Bxd4</b> Black has won the queen for a bishop."),
'0LGTB': ("The pawn on g2 is pinned.",
  "<b>1...Nf3+!</b> The g2 pawn cannot capture, because Black's queen on g5 pins it to the king. The knight move also opens the fifth rank, so the queen now attacks White's queen on d5. After <b>2.Qxf3 Rxf3</b>, Black has won the queen for a knight."),
# ---------- Discovered attacks and deflection ----------
'0RCUP': ("Move the bishop with check and open the e-file.",
  "<b>1.Bxa7+ Kxa7 2.Qxe4</b>. The bishop captures with check, and moving it opens the e-file, so White's queen on e2 now attacks Black's queen on e4. Black has to deal with the check first, and the queen is lost. This is a <i>discovered attack</i>."),
'ATzJl': ("Your queen on f3 is blocked by your own knight.",
  "<b>1.Nf6+ gxf6 2.Qxh5</b>. The knight gives check and gets out of the queen's way, so the queen on f3 now attacks Black's queen on h5. Black must answer the check, and White wins the queen for a knight."),
'cbINZ': ("A check that also opens the second rank.",
  "<b>1.Bg6+ Kxg6 2.Rxa2</b>. The bishop checks the king and uncovers White's rook on e2, which now attacks the black rook on a2. Black takes the bishop, but White takes the rook and wins material."),
'm1HDe': ("Who defends the rook on g6?",
  "<b>1.Rxe7+! Kxe7 2.Qxg6</b>. The rook on g6 is defended twice, by the king and by the knight. The rook takes the knight with check, and after 1...Kxe7 the king has left g6 too, so the queen wins the rook. Removing the defenders like this is called <i>deflection</i>. (If 1...Kg8, 2.Qxg6 wins even more.)"),
'nZ0Yy': ("Your queen on a5 is looking along the fifth rank.",
  "<b>1...Bxg2+ 2.Kxg2 Qxh5</b>. The bishop takes on g2 with check and clears the fifth rank, so the queen on a5 now attacks White's queen on h5. White has to deal with the check, and Black wins the queen."),
'mVvpS': ("Move the bishop with check.",
  "<b>1...Bxh2+ 2.Kxh2 Qxb6</b>. The bishop takes on h2 with check, which opens the sixth rank between Black's queen on e6 and White's queen on b6. After White takes the bishop, Black takes the queen."),
}
